## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph algorithms, DAG analysis, and dynamic programming techniques commonly used in project scheduling and dependency management systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Topological Sorting**: Linear ordering respecting task dependencies
- **Dynamic Programming on DAGs**: Computing optimal values through predecessor relationships
- **Earliest Finish Times**: Maximum of predecessor completion plus task duration
- **Makespan Calculation**: Overall project completion time
- **Complexity**: O(N + E) time and space

## Code Example
```javascript
function makespan(n, durations, edges) {
  // Build adjacency list and compute indegrees
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  // Initialize earliest finish times
  const earliestFinish = durations.slice();
  const queue = [];

  // Start with source nodes (indegree = 0)
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) queue.push(i);
  }

  // Process nodes in topological order
  let head = 0;
  while (head < queue.length) {
    const u = queue[head++];

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      // Update earliest finish for successor
      earliestFinish[v] = Math.max(
        earliestFinish[v],
        earliestFinish[u] + durations[v]
      );

      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) queue.push(v);
    }
  }

  // Return makespan (maximum earliest finish time)
  return Math.max(...earliestFinish);
}
```

## Algorithm Insight
The topological approach ensures each node is processed only after all its predecessors are complete, allowing accurate computation of earliest start times and efficient makespan calculation.

## Why This Question Matters

This problem evaluates a candidate's ability to extend standard grid DP problems with additional state dimensions. Modeling exact-turn constraints is essential in path-planning scenarios such as robot navigation, vehicle routing, and game movement where turn penalties or restrictions exist.

## Core Concepts

- **State Design**: Extending traditional grid DP with turn count and last direction dimensions
- **Transition Logic**: Increment turn count when direction changes between moves
- **Space Optimization**: Using row-wise or 2-layer rolling arrays to reduce memory from O(n*m*K) to O(m*K)
- **Edge Cases**: Handling obstacles, impossible turn requirements, and boundary conditions

## Reference Implementation

```javascript
function maxPathSumWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: m }, () =>
      Array.from({ length: K + 1 }, () => [INF, INF])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === -1) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (j + 1 < m && grid[i][j+1] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (i > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0] = Math.max(
              dp[i][j+1][newTurn][0],
              dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i][j+1]
            );
          }
          dp[i][j+1][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j+1][t][0], dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i][j+1]);
        }
        if (i + 1 < n && grid[i+1][j] !== -1) {
          const newTurn = t + (j > 0 ? 1 : 0);
          if (newTurn <= K) {
            dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1] = Math.max(
              dp[i+1][j][newTurn][1],
              dp[i][j][t][0] + grid[i+1][j]
            );
          }
          dp[i+1][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t][1], dp[i][j][t][1] + grid[i+1][j]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = Math.max(...dp[n-1][m-1].flat());
  return result === INF ? -1 : result;
}
```

